<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wang Chongyang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1113">1113</a>–<a href="/years/1170">1170</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Wang Zhe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wang Chongyang, born Wang Zhe, was a Daoist teacher of the Jin dynasty from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> and the founder of the Quanzhen (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhen-school">Complete Perfection</a>) School. After failing in an official career, he said he had met immortals in 1159 and lived for years as a hermit. In 1167 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, where he gathered seven disciples, the Seven Perfected, who spread his teaching of monastic discipline and inner cultivation across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wang-chongyang.jpg" alt="Wang Chongyang" width="320" height="511" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phoenix Darshan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wang_Chongyang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wang was born Wang Zhongfu into a wealthy family that had been a prominent local clan for generations, and was educated in the Chinese classics and in martial arts. He entered the prefectural school and passed the imperial examination, and early in the Tianjuan era of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xizong-of-jin">Emperor Xizong of Jin</a>, between 1138 and 1140, he came first in the military examination, after which he took the name Dewei. Despite these honours he long served only as a minor official in charge of collecting wine, then resigned and withdrew home, where he spent his days drinking.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to tradition, in the summer of 1159 he met the immortals Zhongli Quan and Lü Dongbin in a tavern in Ganhe Town, near Zhongnan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, and they taught him the secrets of Taoist cultivation. He took the name Zhe and the Taoist name Chongyang, and dug a tomb at the edge of his village near Mount Zhongnan, which he called the Tomb of the Living Dead and marked with a plaque reading Lunatic Wang; he lived there for three years.</p><p>In 1167 he burnt his thatched hut and went east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>. At Ninghai he met Ma Danyang, who built him a hut in the garden of his mansion; Wang named it the Quanzhen Hut, which gave the school its name. Ma's wife Sun Bu'er, Qiu Chuji, who arrived aged nineteen in the ninth month of 1167, Tan Chuduan, Liu Chuxuan, Wang Chuyi and Hao Datong followed. Between 1168 and 1169 he founded five congregations in Shandong, each named for the Three Teachings, since he taught the unity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1169 he set out west with Ma, Tan, Liu and Qiu and died at Daliang, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, on 22 January 1170. The four carried his coffin to his old home in Liujiang Village below Mount Zhongnan, the site Quanzhen revered as the Ancestral Hut, now Chongyang Palace.</p><p>His teaching joined internal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">alchemy</a>, in which the practitioner's own body is the cauldron, with study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daodejing">Tao Te Ching</a>, the Classic of Filial Piety and the Heart Sutra, good deeds, the curbing of desire and monastic life. His writings include the Fifteen Essays to Establish the Teaching and over a thousand poems. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> gave him posthumous titles, and each of his seven disciples founded a lineage, among them Qiu Chuji's Longmen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wang Chongyang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Buddhism enjoyed court patronage, and new Daoist movements arose in Jin territory, above all the Quanzhen (Complete Perfection) school founded by <b>Wang Chongyang</b> in the 1160s, which became one of the two main branches of Daoism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the north, under the Jurchen Jin dynasty, <b>Wang Chongyang</b> founded in 1167 the school of Complete Perfection, Quanzhen, a monastic order of celibate masters that drew on all three teachings.</blockquote><a href="/religion/daoism">Daoism · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Qindu District, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.34,108.71&amp;year=1150" aria-label="Qindu District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7315 920 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Wang Chongyang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7765 1190h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7765 1190h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.34,108.71&amp;year=1150">What was here in 1150? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
